"""In-depth material for the Theory of Computation lessons."""
from learn import deepen

deepen("toc-finite-automata",
       proofs=[
           ("Subset construction: every NFA has an equivalent DFA with at most 2ⁿ states",
            """Let N = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F) be an NFA (first remove ε-moves by working with ε-closures). Define the DFA D with states 2^Q, start state ε-closure({q₀}), transition δ_D(S, a) = ε-closure(⋃_{q∈S} δ(q, a)) and accepting states {S : S ∩ F ≠ ∅}. **Claim:** after reading w, D is in the state S_w = the set of NFA states reachable from q₀ on w. By induction on |w|: for w = ε, S = ε-closure({q₀}); if the claim holds for w then reading a leads the NFA from any state in S_w to δ(q, a) (closed under ε), which is exactly δ_D(S_w, a). D accepts w iff S_w contains an accepting NFA state iff some computation path accepts, so L(D) = L(N). The number of subsets is 2ⁿ. The bound is tight: for 'the n-th symbol from the end is 1' the NFA has n + 1 states but every DFA needs 2ⁿ states."""),
           ("Myhill–Nerode: the number of states of the minimal DFA equals the number of equivalence classes",
            """Define x ~_L y iff for all z, xz ∈ L ⇔ yz ∈ L. **(⇒)** If a DFA with k states accepts L, strings leading to the same state are equivalent, so ~_L has at most k classes. **(⇐)** If ~_L has finitely many classes, build the DFA whose states are the classes, with start class [ε], transition [x] →ᵃ [xa] (well defined because x ~ y implies xa ~ ya) and accepting classes {[x] : x ∈ L}. It accepts L and has exactly as many states as classes, so no smaller DFA exists: the minimal DFA is unique up to renaming. Hence a language is regular iff it has finitely many classes. To show non-regularity, exhibit infinitely many pairwise distinguishable strings: for {aⁿbⁿ}, strings aⁱ and aʲ (i ≠ j) are distinguished by the suffix bⁱ."""),
           ("The number of states for 'the k-th symbol from the end is 1' is 2ᵏ",
            """Let L = {w : the k-th symbol from the right is 1} over {0, 1}. Take two different strings x, y of length k; they differ at some position i. Append a suffix z of length i − 1 (so position i of x or y becomes the k-th symbol from the right): xz has the k-th-from-the-end symbol xᵢ, and yz has yᵢ, so exactly one of xz, yz is in L. Hence all 2ᵏ strings of length k are pairwise distinguishable, so any DFA needs at least 2ᵏ states; the DFA that remembers the last k symbols uses exactly 2ᵏ."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Designing DFAs by remembering information",
            """Decide what the automaton must remember about the prefix read so far and make that the state. 'Contains the substring 011': the longest suffix of the input that is a prefix of 011 (four states ε, 0, 01, 011 with the last absorbing). 'Binary number divisible by k': the remainder r, with transition r → (2r + b) mod k (k states). 'Number of 0s is a multiple of 3 and number of 1s even': the pair of counts, 3 × 2 = 6 states (product construction). Complement (swap accepting states of a **complete** DFA), union and intersection (product automaton, |Q₁|·|Q₂| states), reversal (reverse edges; may need subset construction), prefix and suffix closure."""),
           ("Moore, Mealy and the cost of conversions",
            """A Moore machine's output depends on the state only (n + 1 outputs for n inputs when the initial output counts), a Mealy machine's on state and input; a Mealy machine with n states can be converted to a Moore machine with at most n × |outputs| states. NFA to DFA may blow up exponentially; ε-NFA to NFA keeps the same number of states; regular expression of length m gives an ε-NFA with at most 2m states (Thompson); DFA to regular expression can blow up exponentially in length. Minimising a DFA with n states takes O(n²) with table filling, O(n log n) with Hopcroft's algorithm."""),
       ])

deepen("toc-regular-expressions",
       proofs=[
           ("Arden's rule: if R = Q + RP with ε ∉ P then R = QP*",
            """**Solution.** Substitute R = QP* into the right side: Q + (QP*)P = Q(ε + P*P) = QP* = R, so QP* satisfies the equation. **Uniqueness (needs ε ∉ P).** Suppose R satisfies R = Q + RP. Iterating gives R = Q + QP + RP² = Q(ε + P + … + Pᵏ) + RPᵏ⁺¹. Take a string w ∈ R of length m. Because ε ∉ P every string in Pᵏ⁺¹ has length at least k + 1; choosing k = m makes the term RPᵏ⁺¹ unable to contain w, so w ∈ Q(ε + P + … + Pᵏ) ⊆ QP*. Conversely QP* ⊆ R from the iteration. So R = QP*. This is the engine behind converting a DFA to a regular expression by solving the state equations (qᵢ = Σ qⱼa + [ε if accepting])."""),
           ("Kleene star identities: (r + s)* = (r*s*)* and (rs)*r = r(sr)*",
            """For (r + s)* ⊆ (r*s*)*: any string in the left is a concatenation of strings each from r or from s; each such piece is itself in r*s* (take the other star empty), so the concatenation lies in (r*s*)*. For (r*s*)* ⊆ (r + s)*: every string of r*s* is a concatenation of r-strings then s-strings, hence in (r + s)*, and (r + s)* is closed under concatenation. For the shifting identity, a string of (rs)*r is r₁s₁r₂s₂…rₖsₖrₖ₊₁ with k ≥ 0, which regroups as r₁(s₁r₂)(s₂r₃)…(sₖrₖ₊₁) ∈ r(sr)*; the reverse inclusion is the same regrouping. Non-identities: (r + s)* ≠ r* + s* because it contains mixed strings such as ab, and (rs)* ≠ r*s* because the latter contains rr."""),
           ("Regular languages are closed under complement and intersection",
            """Let D be a **complete** DFA for L. Swapping accepting and non-accepting states gives a DFA for the complement: a string ends in an accepting state of the new machine iff it ended in a non-accepting state of D (completeness guarantees there is exactly one computation). For intersection use De Morgan: L₁ ∩ L₂ = (L₁ᶜ ∪ L₂ᶜ)ᶜ, with closure under union given by the regular-expression r + s (or the product automaton). The product construction directly gives a DFA with |Q₁|·|Q₂| states, accepting when both components accept. Closure under reversal: reverse every edge of an NFA, swap start and accepting sets (adding ε-moves from a new start state if several former accepting states exist)."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("From automaton to expression and from grammar to automaton",
            """State elimination: add a new start and accept state, then remove states one by one, replacing paths through the removed state q by edges labelled R_in · R_loop* · R_out (union with parallel edges). A right-linear grammar A → aB | b | ε gives an NFA with a state per non-terminal; a left-linear grammar is the mirror image (reverse the language). A grammar that mixes left- and right-linear rules can generate non-regular languages such as {aⁿbⁿ}. Regular expressions can describe infinite languages with finite descriptions; every finite language is regular; the number of strings of length n in a*b* is n + 1."""),
           ("Decision problems for regular languages",
            """Membership: simulate the DFA in O(n). Emptiness: reachability of an accepting state. Finiteness: look for a cycle on a path from the start to an accepting state (equivalently, an accepted string with length in [p, 2p) where p is the number of DFA states). Equivalence: minimise both DFAs and compare, or test emptiness of the symmetric difference. Containment L₁ ⊆ L₂: L₁ ∩ L₂ᶜ empty. Universality: complement empty. All are decidable in polynomial time for DFAs and PSPACE-complete for NFAs/regular expressions."""),
       ])

deepen("toc-cfg-pda",
       proofs=[
           ("A CNF derivation of a string of length n takes 2n − 1 steps",
            """In Chomsky normal form every production is A → BC or A → a. A derivation tree for w with |w| = n ≥ 1 has n leaves. Internal nodes using A → BC are binary; an inductive count shows a full binary tree with n leaves has n − 1 binary internal nodes. Besides these, each of the n leaves is produced by a rule A → a, one more step each. Total number of derivation steps = (n − 1) + n = **2n − 1**. In Greibach normal form (A → aα) every step produces exactly one terminal, so a derivation of w takes exactly n steps. These counts are frequently asked."""),
           ("Equivalence of CFGs and PDAs (CFG ⇒ PDA)",
            """Given a CFG G, build a one-state PDA that simulates leftmost derivations on its stack. Start by pushing S. Repeat: if the top is a variable A, nondeterministically choose a production A → α and replace A by α (pushing α's symbols so that the first is on top); if the top is a terminal a, it must match the next input symbol, then pop it. Accept when the stack is empty and the input is consumed. Each accepting computation traces a leftmost derivation of the input and conversely every leftmost derivation yields such a computation, so L(PDA) = L(G). The reverse construction (PDA ⇒ CFG) uses variables A_{pq} meaning 'from state p with a given stack top to state q having popped it', and is polynomial in the PDA size."""),
           ("Why an ambiguous grammar is not LL(1) or LR(1), and why the dangling else is ambiguous",
            """An LL(1) or LR(1) parser decides every step deterministically from one look-ahead token, so it builds exactly one parse tree for each input; two different trees would require two different choices at some step with the same look-ahead, contradicting determinism. Hence every LL(1)/LR(1) grammar is unambiguous. In S → iEtS | iEtSeS | a the string i E t i E t a e a has two trees depending on which `if` owns the `else`; the parser table entry for (S, i) holds two productions, a conflict. Ambiguity can be removed by rewriting (matched/unmatched statements) or resolved by precedence rules ('else binds to the nearest if')."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Standard CFGs and simplification",
            """Balanced parentheses S → (S)S | ε; {aⁿbⁿ} S → aSb | ε; palindromes S → aSa | bSb | a | b | ε; equal numbers of a and b S → aSbS | bSaS | ε; {aⁿbᵐ : n ≠ m} is generated by S → aSb | A | B with A → aA | a, B → bB | b. Simplification order: remove ε-productions, then unit productions, then useless symbols (non-generating, then unreachable) — the order matters. Left recursion elimination turns A → Aα | β into A → βA′, A′ → αA′ | ε. CNF conversion first needs the simplification, then breaking long right sides and isolating terminals."""),
           ("Pushdown automata variants",
            """A PDA may accept by final state or by empty stack (equivalent in power for nondeterministic PDAs; for deterministic PDAs final-state acceptance is more powerful). Deterministic PDAs recognise exactly the DCFLs, a strict subset of CFLs; palindromes wwᴿ need nondeterminism (the middle must be guessed) whereas wcwᴿ is deterministic. A PDA with two stacks equals a Turing machine. A one-counter PDA recognises {aⁿbⁿ}. CFLs are closed under union, concatenation, star, reversal, homomorphism and intersection with regular languages but not under intersection or complement."""),
       ])

deepen("toc-closure-pumping",
       proofs=[
           ("The pumping lemma for regular languages",
            """Let M be a DFA with p states for the regular language L and let w ∈ L with |w| ≥ p. Consider the p + 1 states visited while reading the first p symbols (including the start). By the pigeonhole principle two of them are equal, say after reading i and j symbols with i < j ≤ p. Let x = w₁…wᵢ, y = wᵢ₊₁…wⱼ (|y| ≥ 1) and z the rest. Reading y from the repeated state returns to the same state, so the loop can be traversed any number of times: xyᵏz is accepted for every k ≥ 0 (k = 0 skips the loop, k > 1 repeats it). Also |xy| = j ≤ p. This is the lemma. It is a necessary condition: it can prove that a language is not regular but never that it is regular."""),
           ("{aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0} and {a^(n²)} are not regular",
            """Suppose {aⁿbⁿ} were regular with pumping length p. Take w = aᵖbᵖ. Since |xy| ≤ p, y consists only of a's: y = aᵏ with k ≥ 1. Then xy²z = aᵖ⁺ᵏbᵖ has more a's than b's and is not in the language — contradiction. For L = {a^(n²)} take w = a^(p²) and pump once with |y| = k, 1 ≤ k ≤ p: the new length p² + k satisfies p² < p² + k ≤ p² + p < (p + 1)², strictly between consecutive squares, so it is not a square — contradiction. For primes take w = a^q with q prime and q ≥ p + 2 and write y = aᵏ; pumping q times gives length q + (q)·k = q(1 + k), a composite number because both factors exceed 1, so the language is not regular."""),
           ("The pumping lemma for context-free languages and why aⁿbⁿcⁿ is not context-free",
            """If L is context-free with pumping length p, every w ∈ L with |w| ≥ p splits as uvxyz with |vxy| ≤ p, |vy| ≥ 1 and uvⁱxyⁱz ∈ L for all i ≥ 0. (Reason: a parse tree of a long string has a path with a repeated variable A; the two subtrees give the decomposition and the part between the two occurrences can be repeated or removed.) For w = aᵖbᵖcᵖ, the window vxy has length ≤ p so it cannot contain both an a and a c; hence pumping changes at most two of the three symbols' counts but not the third, e.g. uv²xy²z has unbalanced counts. So aⁿbⁿcⁿ is not context-free. It follows that CFLs are not closed under intersection since {aⁿbⁿcᵐ} ∩ {aᵐbⁿcⁿ} = {aⁿbⁿcⁿ}, and not under complement (otherwise union and complement would give intersection)."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Choosing between the tools",
            """To prove regular: build a DFA/NFA/regular expression or use closure properties. To prove non-regular: pumping lemma (choose a clever w and a clever i), Myhill–Nerode (infinitely many distinguishable strings), or closure (if L were regular then L ∩ a*b* = {aⁿbⁿ} would be regular). Languages that look hard but are regular: {aᵐbⁿ}, strings with an equal number of occurrences of the substrings 01 and 10 (these always differ by at most one so the language is regular), binary numbers divisible by 5. Not regular: {ww}, palindromes, {aⁿbᵐ : n > m}, {aᵖ : p prime}, strings with equal numbers of 0s and 1s."""),
           ("Closure table reminder",
            """Regular: closed under everything common (∪, ∩, complement, concatenation, star, reversal, difference, homomorphism, inverse homomorphism, quotients). CFL: ∪, concatenation, star, reversal, homomorphism, inverse homomorphism, intersection with regular; not ∩, complement, difference. DCFL: complement and intersection with regular; not ∪, ∩, concatenation, star, reversal. Recursive: ∪, ∩, complement, concatenation, star. RE: ∪, ∩, concatenation, star, homomorphism; not complement."""),
       ])

deepen("toc-turing-decidability",
       proofs=[
           ("The halting problem is undecidable (diagonalisation)",
            """Suppose a machine H decides HALT = {⟨M, w⟩ : M halts on w}: H halts on every input and accepts exactly when M halts on w. Build a machine D that on input ⟨M⟩ runs H on ⟨M, ⟨M⟩⟩ and then does the opposite: if H says 'M halts on its own description' D loops forever; if H says 'M does not halt' D halts. Now run D on its own description ⟨D⟩. If D halts on ⟨D⟩ then H said 'halts', so D loops — contradiction; if D does not halt on ⟨D⟩ then H said 'does not halt', so D halts — contradiction. Therefore H cannot exist. HALT is nevertheless recursively enumerable: simulate M on w and accept if it halts."""),
           ("A language is recursive iff both it and its complement are recursively enumerable",
            """If L is recursive, a decider for L accepts exactly L; swapping its accept and reject answers gives a decider for the complement, so both L and Lᶜ are decidable and hence RE. Conversely suppose M₁ recognises L and M₂ recognises Lᶜ. Given input w run M₁ and M₂ in parallel (alternate steps). Exactly one of them accepts w (since w is in exactly one of L and Lᶜ), and it does so after finitely many steps. If M₁ accepts, answer yes; if M₂ accepts, answer no. This always halts, so L is recursive. Consequence: the complement of HALT is not RE (otherwise HALT would be decidable)."""),
           ("Rice's theorem and reductions",
            """**Reduction principle:** if A ≤ₘ B via a computable f (w ∈ A ⇔ f(w) ∈ B) and B is decidable, then A is decidable (compute f(w) and ask the decider for B). Contrapositive: if A is undecidable then B is undecidable. **Rice:** let P be a non-trivial property of the language of a TM (some machines have it, some do not). Assume without loss of generality that the empty-language machine does not have P and let M_P be some machine whose language has P. Reduce A_TM to P: given ⟨M, w⟩ build M′ that on input x first runs M on w; if M accepts, it then runs M_P on x and answers as M_P does; otherwise it never accepts. Then L(M′) = L(M_P) (has P) if M accepts w and ∅ (does not have P) otherwise. A decider for P would decide A_TM — impossible. Hence every non-trivial semantic property (emptiness, finiteness, regularity, 'accepts ε') is undecidable."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Machine models and the hierarchy",
            """Multi-tape, two-way infinite-tape and nondeterministic TMs recognise the same languages as the basic model (a multi-tape machine runs in quadratic slowdown on a single tape, a nondeterministic one with exponential slowdown by breadth-first search of the computation tree). Church–Turing thesis: every effectively computable function is computable by a TM (not provable, a thesis). Hierarchy: Regular ⊂ DCFL ⊂ CFL ⊂ CSL (linear bounded automata) ⊂ Recursive ⊂ RE; there are languages that are not even RE (the complement of HALT, a diagonal language) — countably many TMs versus uncountably many languages."""),
           ("Classic undecidable and decidable problems",
            """Undecidable: halting, A_TM, emptiness of a TM's language, equivalence of two TMs, Post correspondence problem, ambiguity and equivalence of CFGs, universality of a CFG, whether a CFG generates all strings, whether L(G₁) ∩ L(G₂) = ∅, totality. Decidable: membership, emptiness, finiteness for CFGs and regular languages, equivalence of DFAs, acceptance by an LBA (A_LBA), emptiness for regular languages. Semi-decidable (RE but not decidable): HALT, A_TM, non-emptiness of a TM language. Co-RE: emptiness, equivalence of CFGs."""),
       ])
